---
title: "Five Times the Record Proved Us Wrong"
description: "Every project starts with a guess about how people will behave. The useful part is when the record disagrees."
date: 2026-10-02
draft: true
---

Every project starts with a guess about how people will behave. The most useful thing a record does is tell you when the guess was wrong. Here are five times it told us.

## Launch night is not the point

At Kasina × Crocs in Seoul we built for launch night, because launch night is the photograph. The record said otherwise. The public weekend converted harder: 51% opted into marketing, against 38% on opening night. Launch night amplifies. The public days build the audience.

## Fewer people, better people

With H.LLS we assumed digital would do the volume and physical the texture. Half right. Physical taps were a small share of the audience, but the fans who showed up most sat almost entirely in that group. An object someone carried through a show predicted commitment in a way no click did.

## Put the tag where the hand goes

At G-SHOCK × Who Eats Art in Singapore, we expected the artwork to spread attention across all eleven tags. Three placements produced 70% of participants, and one produced none. The technology was identical at every point. What varied was whether a person could see the tag and reach it.

## The tag is half a system

On the Navinder Nangla drop at END., 81% of entries arrived on one day: the live window painting. After that they trickled, then stopped. The tag worked the whole time; what ran out was the reason to look. The other half of the system is a calendar.

## People want to be let in, not checked

The E Gucewicz Genesis frames began as an authentication brief. Testing said something else: people did not want the frame to prove itself, they wanted it to let them in. We rebuilt the idea around access, and that finding has shaped every deployment since.

## Why it matters

None of these would have shown up in a survey or a footfall estimate. They showed up because every tap was a real moment, with a time and a place on it. That is the point of keeping the record: not to prove you were right, but to find out where you were not, while there is still time to make the next one better.
